The scaling framework implemented in src/ follows:
Ran Duan, Seth Pettie, Hsin-Hao Su. Scaling Algorithms for Weighted Matching
in General Graphs. ACM Transactions on Algorithms 14(1), Article 8, 2018.
DOI: https://doi.org/10.1145/3155301
Full reference: https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.1919v6

Parts of the standard Edmonds forest tracing and nested alternating-cycle
rotation/path lifting in src/search.rs and src/blossom.rs are adapted from
NetworkX max_weight_matching. Its BSD-3-Clause copyright and license notices
are retained in LICENSE. The scaling, liquidation, criteria, reweighting,
perfection, API, and test harness integrate these into the paper's scaling
framework. NetworkX does not implement this scaling driver.

The original source repository included vendor/mwmatching-0.1.1 as a TEST-ONLY independent Edmonds/Galil solver by
Andrew Henshaw, reimplementing Joris van Rantwijk's Python implementation.
The upstream crate declares license MIT in its Cargo.toml. Its source header
and metadata are retained. Rustfmt has only reformatted this vendored code;
no algorithmic changes have been made. Upstream crate:
https://static.crates.io/crates/mwmatching/mwmatching-0.1.1.crate
Repository: https://github.com/ahenshaw/mwmatching
The Blossom-Study copy does not include this vendored solver.
The test wrapper drops negative edges for unconstrained matching and shifts
weights when requesting maximum cardinality to avoid the old reference's
all-negative initialization corner case. Original weights are compared.

Python NetworkX is optionally used as a THIRD differential oracle by
 tests/networkx_check.py; it is not a Rust dependency.
